==Norwegian==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|no|Eirik}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|no}} {{term|Eiríkr|lang=non}}, of Proto-Norse *Aiwa-rīkiaR ("forever rich/powerful").

===Proper noun===
{{no-proper noun}}

# {{given name|male|lang=no}}.

====Usage notes====
* One of the most common given names in Norway since the Middle Ages.

====Related terms====
* (''female names'') {{l|no|Erika}}
* (''surnames'') {{l|no|Eriksen}}

===References===
* Kristoffer Kruken - Ola Stemshaug: Norsk personnamnleksikon, Det Norske Samlaget, Oslo 1995, ISBN 82-521-4483-7
* [http://www.ssb.no/navn/ ] Statistisk sentralbyrå, Namnestatistikk: 20 608 males with the given name Erik (compared to 12 953 named Eirik) living in Norway on January 1st 2011, with a frequency varying between 0.4 - 1.0 percent of the gender group without clear peaks. Accessed on April 29th, 2011.

